# dyn.md D-26, the sizes of `.gnu.hash` for n hashed entries, n = 0, 1,
# 2, 3, 10, 100, 1000, 5000: one program per n, which exports
# `cb_0000`..`cb_<n-1>` because the library references all 5000 names
# (weakly, so the program may define only some of them). The essential
# claim is in the run: the library's 5000 references are resolved by the
# loader through the program's table (n of them to the program's
# definitions, the others to nothing), each resolved one calls, `dlsym`
# agrees with the loader on all 5000 names, and the program's own lookup
# of every hashed entry through its table (Bloom word, bucket, chain:
# `dynwalk.h`) finds it.
#
# The sizes, read by `llvm-readelf --gnu-hash-table`: mortar's are lld's
# closed form, `nbuckets = max(1, n/4)`, `maskwords` the least power of two
# above `12n/64` (1 for 0), `shift` 26, `symoffset` the first hashed
# entry; with n = 0 one empty bucket, one zero mask word, `symoffset` the
# `.dynsym` count. Where the references differ (dyn.md §13.1, "Bloom
# words, buckets"): GNU ld uses a prime number of buckets, a
# size-dependent shift and `symoffset` 1 for an empty table, and hashes
# the x86-64 PIE's `__cxa_finalize` (one more hashed entry); mold has
# `n/8 + 1` buckets. Each is held to its own sizes below; all three
# tables find every name.
#@ spec: dyn.md "D-26"
#@ arch: aarch64 x86_64
#@ requires: llvm-readelf
#@ fixture: dynsym-walk

cc -O1 -fPIC lib.c -o lib.o
shlib gcc lib.o -Wl,-soname,libcbs.so -o libcbs.so
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=0 main.c -o m0.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=1 main.c -o m1.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=2 main.c -o m2.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=3 main.c -o m3.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=10 main.c -o m10.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=100 main.c -o m100.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=1000 main.c -o m1000.o
cc -O0 -fPIE -DN=5000 main.c -o m5000.o

link gcc -pie m0.o -L. -lcbs -o g0
run g0
  stdout: n=0: the table finds 0 of 0 hashed entries
  stdout: library: resolved 0 of 5000, called 0, dlsym agrees 5000, absent rejected 5000
check llvm-readelf --gnu-hash-table g0
  has(lld,mortar): Num Buckets: 1$
  has(lld,mortar): Num Mask Words: 1$
  has(lld,mortar): Shift Count: 26$
  has(mold): Num Buckets: 1$
  has(mold): Num Mask Words: 1$
  has(bfd,aarch64): Num Buckets: 1$
  has(bfd,aarch64): Num Mask Words: 1$
  has(bfd,aarch64): Shift Count: 0$
  has(bfd,x86_64): Num Buckets: 2$
  has(bfd,x86_64): Num Mask Words: 1$
  has(bfd,x86_64): Shift Count: 6$
  has(lld,mortar,aarch64): First Hashed Symbol Index: 13$
  has(lld,mortar,x86_64): First Hashed Symbol Index: 12$
  has(bfd,aarch64): First Hashed Symbol Index: 1$

check(lld,mortar,aarch64) readelf --dyn-syms -W g0
  has: Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 13 entries
check(lld,mortar,x86_64) readelf --dyn-syms -W g0
  has: Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 12 entries

